{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scatterplots answer how two quantitative measures vary together. Position\ncarries the primary evidence. Color, radius, a fitted line, or chronological\nconnections should add one clearly stated dimension rather than compete with\nthat relationship."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"choose-the-comparison","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose the comparison"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reader question"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start with"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do two quantitative measures move together?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A scatterplot"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What linear tendency summarizes that relationship?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scatterplot plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"linearRegressionY"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How does the relationship evolve in a known order?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A connected scatterplot"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Does a series depend on its previous observation?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A lag plot"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Which dense point is closest to the pointer or keyboard cursor?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A scatterplot with a spatial focus strategy"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not infer causation from proximity or a fitted trend. Show the model and\npreparation only when they answer the stated question."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"start-with-two-quantitative-measures","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start with two quantitative measures"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A plain scatterplot should establish the relationship before adding a fitted\nmodel, chronology, or spatial partition."}]},{"type":"component","name":"live-example","tagName":"md-live-example","attributes":{"id":"basic-scatter-1"},"properties":{"data-example-group":"basic-scatter","data-collapsed-indexes":"[]","data-workspace":"{\"version\":1,\"entry\":\"/src/chart.ts\",\"environment\":\"charts\",\"files\":{\"/src/chart.ts\":\"import { defineChart, dot } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\\n\\nconst rows = [\\n  { temperature: 12, sales: 18 },\\n  { temperature: 16, sales: 25 },\\n  { temperature: 20, sales: 31 },\\n  { temperature: 24, sales: 46 },\\n  { temperature: 29, sales: 52 },\\n  { temperature: 32, sales: 61 },\\n]\\n\\nconst chart = defineChart({\\n  marks: [\\n    dot(rows, {\\n      x: 'temperature',\\n      y: 'sales',\\n      r: 5,\\n      fill: '#2563eb',\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n  scales: {\\n    x: {\\n      scale: scaleLinear,\\n      grid: true,\\n      axis: { label: 'Temperature (°C)' },\\n    },\\n    y: {\\n      scale: scaleLinear,\\n      grid: true,\\n      axis: { label: 'Daily sales' },\\n    },\\n  },\\n})\\n\\nexport default chart\"}}"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { defineChart, dot } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst rows = [\n  { temperature: 12, sales: 18 },\n  { temperature: 16, sales: 25 },\n  { temperature: 20, sales: 31 },\n  { temperature: 24, sales: 46 },\n  { temperature: 29, sales: 52 },\n  { temperature: 32, sales: 61 },\n]\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    dot(rows, {\n      x: 'temperature',\n      y: 'sales',\n      r: 5,\n      fill: '#2563eb',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Temperature (°C)' },\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Daily sales' },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\nexport default chart","lang":"ts","meta":"group=basic-scatter env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry","title":"/src/chart.ts","file":"/src/chart.ts"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"add-a-linear-regression","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add a linear regression"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass the observations directly to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"linearRegressionY"},{"type":"text","value":". The mark owns the\nleast-squares fit, semantic-domain samples, optional confidence band, and\naggregate source lineage. Keep the dot layer separate so each observation\nremains independently focusable."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"31-linear-regression","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ci: 0"},{"type":"text","value":" when only the fitted line is needed. The default "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0.95"},{"type":"text","value":" band uses a\nStudent-t interval for the fitted mean. See the\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/regression.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"linear regression mark"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for grouping,\nsampling, and degenerate-fit behavior.\nThe dot layer still preserves every observation while the regression mark owns\nonly its derived model geometry."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"connect-observations-only-when-order-matters","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Connect observations only when order matters"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A connected scatterplot turns sequence into a path through two-dimensional\nmeasure space. Chronological labels and direction arrows make that additional\nordering visible."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"56-connected-scatter","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Without an explicit order, connecting points invents a relationship. Keep the\npath, arrow, selected labels, and points as separate layers so each can use the\nsame scales without sharing renderer-specific state."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"compare-each-observation-with-its-predecessor","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compare each observation with its predecessor"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A lag plot moves time out of the axis and into data preparation. Each point\npairs a current value with the previous value; an identity rule shows where\nthose values would be equal."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"60-lag-autocorrelation","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Make the lag length explicit and decide how the first observation is handled.\nThe chart should receive the resulting pairs rather than conceal the shift\ninside a mark."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"separate-visible-cells-from-nearest-point-focus","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Separate visible cells from nearest-point focus"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Voronoi cells make each point's nearest region visible. The optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"voronoi"},{"type":"text","value":"\nmark paints those cells but deliberately adds no focus candidates. A layered\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dot"},{"type":"text","value":" mark remains the semantic source for pointer focus, keyboard navigation,\nand tooltips."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"65-voronoi-nearest-tooltip","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/voronoi.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"voronoi"},{"type":"text","value":" mark"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for final-screen cell\ngeometry and stable identity. "},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/tooltips-and-focus.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tooltips and Focus"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\ndefines the focus and formatting model. Use a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartSpatialIndexFactory"},{"type":"text","value":" when\nlookup performance matters but the cells should not be painted."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"production-checks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Production checks"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use quantitative scales with intentional domains on both axes. Use a\nlogarithmic scale only when multiplicative distance is the intended reading;\nsee "},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/scales-and-d3.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scales"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Map magnitude through an area-preserving radial scale when point size carries\na third quantitative value."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Control opacity or aggregate spatially before thousands of overlapping dots\nobscure the distribution. See "},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/large-data.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Large Data"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep lag pairs in data preparation. Keep lookup-only spatial indexes in\ninteraction capabilities; use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"voronoi"},{"type":"text","value":" only when cells are part of the\nvisible encoding."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provide keyboard-equivalent focus and a textual value path, as described in\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/accessibility.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accessibility"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The channel and styling contracts for points are in\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/dot-and-hexagon.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dot and Hexagon Marks"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"choose-the-comparison","text":"Choose the comparison","level":2},{"id":"start-with-two-quantitative-measures","text":"Start with two quantitative measures","level":2},{"id":"add-a-linear-regression","text":"Add a linear regression","level":2},{"id":"connect-observations-only-when-order-matters","text":"Connect observations only when order matters","level":2},{"id":"compare-each-observation-with-its-predecessor","text":"Compare each observation with its predecessor","level":2},{"id":"separate-visible-cells-from-nearest-point-focus","text":"Separate visible cells from nearest-point focus","level":2},{"id":"production-checks","text":"Production checks","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nScatterplots answer how two quantitative measures vary together. Position\ncarries the primary evidence. Color, radius, a fitted line, or chronological\nconnections should add one clearly stated dimension rather than compete with\nthat relationship.\n\n## Choose the comparison\n\n| Reader question                                                 | Start with                                  |\n| --------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |\n| Do two quantitative measures move together?                     | A scatterplot                               |\n| What linear tendency summarizes that relationship?              | Scatterplot plus `linearRegressionY`        |\n| How does the relationship evolve in a known order?              | A connected scatterplot                     |\n| Does a series depend on its previous observation?               | A lag plot                                  |\n| Which dense point is closest to the pointer or keyboard cursor? | A scatterplot with a spatial focus strategy |\n\nDo not infer causation from proximity or a fitted trend. Show the model and\npreparation only when they answer the stated question.\n\n## Start with two quantitative measures\n\nA plain scatterplot should establish the relationship before adding a fitted\nmodel, chronology, or spatial partition.\n\n```ts group=basic-scatter env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart, dot } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst rows = [\n  { temperature: 12, sales: 18 },\n  { temperature: 16, sales: 25 },\n  { temperature: 20, sales: 31 },\n  { temperature: 24, sales: 46 },\n  { temperature: 29, sales: 52 },\n  { temperature: 32, sales: 61 },\n]\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    dot(rows, {\n      x: 'temperature',\n      y: 'sales',\n      r: 5,\n      fill: '#2563eb',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Temperature (°C)' },\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Daily sales' },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\nexport default chart\n```\n\n## Add a linear regression\n\nPass the observations directly to `linearRegressionY`. The mark owns the\nleast-squares fit, semantic-domain samples, optional confidence band, and\naggregate source lineage. Keep the dot layer separate so each observation\nremains independently focusable.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=31-linear-regression height=480 -->\n\nSet `ci: 0` when only the fitted line is needed. The default `0.95` band uses a\nStudent-t interval for the fitted mean. See the\n[linear regression mark](../reference/marks/regression.md) for grouping,\nsampling, and degenerate-fit behavior.\nThe dot layer still preserves every observation while the regression mark owns\nonly its derived model geometry.\n\n## Connect observations only when order matters\n\nA connected scatterplot turns sequence into a path through two-dimensional\nmeasure space. Chronological labels and direction arrows make that additional\nordering visible.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=56-connected-scatter height=480 -->\n\nWithout an explicit order, connecting points invents a relationship. Keep the\npath, arrow, selected labels, and points as separate layers so each can use the\nsame scales without sharing renderer-specific state.\n\n## Compare each observation with its predecessor\n\nA lag plot moves time out of the axis and into data preparation. Each point\npairs a current value with the previous value; an identity rule shows where\nthose values would be equal.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=60-lag-autocorrelation height=480 -->\n\nMake the lag length explicit and decide how the first observation is handled.\nThe chart should receive the resulting pairs rather than conceal the shift\ninside a mark.\n\n## Separate visible cells from nearest-point focus\n\nVoronoi cells make each point's nearest region visible. The optional `voronoi`\nmark paints those cells but deliberately adds no focus candidates. A layered\n`dot` mark remains the semantic source for pointer focus, keyboard navigation,\nand tooltips.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=65-voronoi-nearest-tooltip height=480 -->\n\nSee the [`voronoi` mark](../reference/marks/voronoi.md) for final-screen cell\ngeometry and stable identity. [Tooltips and Focus](../guides/tooltips-and-focus.md)\ndefines the focus and formatting model. Use a `ChartSpatialIndexFactory` when\nlookup performance matters but the cells should not be painted.\n\n## Production checks\n\n- Use quantitative scales with intentional domains on both axes. Use a\n  logarithmic scale only when multiplicative distance is the intended reading;\n  see [Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md).\n- Map magnitude through an area-preserving radial scale when point size carries\n  a third quantitative value.\n- Control opacity or aggregate spatially before thousands of overlapping dots\n  obscure the distribution. See [Large Data](../guides/large-data.md).\n- Keep lag pairs in data preparation. Keep lookup-only spatial indexes in\n  interaction capabilities; use `voronoi` only when cells are part of the\n  visible encoding.\n- Provide keyboard-equivalent focus and a textual value path, as described in\n  [Accessibility](../guides/accessibility.md).\n\nThe channel and styling contracts for points are in\n[Dot and Hexagon Marks](../reference/marks/dot-and-hexagon.md).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Scatterplots and Relationships\ndescription: Choose scatterplot, regression, connected-path, lag, and nearest-point patterns for quantitative relationships.\n---\n\nScatterplots answer how two quantitative measures vary together. Position\ncarries the primary evidence. Color, radius, a fitted line, or chronological\nconnections should add one clearly stated dimension rather than compete with\nthat relationship.\n\n## Choose the comparison\n\n| Reader question                                                 | Start with                                  |\n| --------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |\n| Do two quantitative measures move together?                     | A scatterplot                               |\n| What linear tendency summarizes that relationship?              | Scatterplot plus `linearRegressionY`        |\n| How does the relationship evolve in a known order?              | A connected scatterplot                     |\n| Does a series depend on its previous observation?               | A lag plot                                  |\n| Which dense point is closest to the pointer or keyboard cursor? | A scatterplot with a spatial focus strategy |\n\nDo not infer causation from proximity or a fitted trend. Show the model and\npreparation only when they answer the stated question.\n\n## Start with two quantitative measures\n\nA plain scatterplot should establish the relationship before adding a fitted\nmodel, chronology, or spatial partition.\n\n```ts group=basic-scatter env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart, dot } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst rows = [\n  { temperature: 12, sales: 18 },\n  { temperature: 16, sales: 25 },\n  { temperature: 20, sales: 31 },\n  { temperature: 24, sales: 46 },\n  { temperature: 29, sales: 52 },\n  { temperature: 32, sales: 61 },\n]\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    dot(rows, {\n      x: 'temperature',\n      y: 'sales',\n      r: 5,\n      fill: '#2563eb',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Temperature (°C)' },\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Daily sales' },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\nexport default chart\n```\n\n## Add a linear regression\n\nPass the observations directly to `linearRegressionY`. The mark owns the\nleast-squares fit, semantic-domain samples, optional confidence band, and\naggregate source lineage. Keep the dot layer separate so each observation\nremains independently focusable.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=31-linear-regression height=480 -->\n\nSet `ci: 0` when only the fitted line is needed. The default `0.95` band uses a\nStudent-t interval for the fitted mean. See the\n[linear regression mark](../reference/marks/regression.md) for grouping,\nsampling, and degenerate-fit behavior.\nThe dot layer still preserves every observation while the regression mark owns\nonly its derived model geometry.\n\n## Connect observations only when order matters\n\nA connected scatterplot turns sequence into a path through two-dimensional\nmeasure space. Chronological labels and direction arrows make that additional\nordering visible.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=56-connected-scatter height=480 -->\n\nWithout an explicit order, connecting points invents a relationship. Keep the\npath, arrow, selected labels, and points as separate layers so each can use the\nsame scales without sharing renderer-specific state.\n\n## Compare each observation with its predecessor\n\nA lag plot moves time out of the axis and into data preparation. Each point\npairs a current value with the previous value; an identity rule shows where\nthose values would be equal.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=60-lag-autocorrelation height=480 -->\n\nMake the lag length explicit and decide how the first observation is handled.\nThe chart should receive the resulting pairs rather than conceal the shift\ninside a mark.\n\n## Separate visible cells from nearest-point focus\n\nVoronoi cells make each point's nearest region visible. The optional `voronoi`\nmark paints those cells but deliberately adds no focus candidates. A layered\n`dot` mark remains the semantic source for pointer focus, keyboard navigation,\nand tooltips.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=65-voronoi-nearest-tooltip height=480 -->\n\nSee the [`voronoi` mark](../reference/marks/voronoi.md) for final-screen cell\ngeometry and stable identity. [Tooltips and Focus](../guides/tooltips-and-focus.md)\ndefines the focus and formatting model. Use a `ChartSpatialIndexFactory` when\nlookup performance matters but the cells should not be painted.\n\n## Production checks\n\n- Use quantitative scales with intentional domains on both axes. Use a\n  logarithmic scale only when multiplicative distance is the intended reading;\n  see [Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md).\n- Map magnitude through an area-preserving radial scale when point size carries\n  a third quantitative value.\n- Control opacity or aggregate spatially before thousands of overlapping dots\n  obscure the distribution. See [Large Data](../guides/large-data.md).\n- Keep lag pairs in data preparation. Keep lookup-only spatial indexes in\n  interaction capabilities; use `voronoi` only when cells are part of the\n  visible encoding.\n- Provide keyboard-equivalent focus and a textual value path, as described in\n  [Accessibility](../guides/accessibility.md).\n\nThe channel and styling contracts for points are in\n[Dot and Hexagon Marks](../reference/marks/dot-and-hexagon.md).\n"}
